ence.","options":["The quality of the samples were satisfactory.","The quality of the samples was satisfactory.","Despite of the delay, the quality of the samples was satisfactory.","The team discussed about why the quality of the samples was satisfactory."],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The first sentence follows standard agreement, tense and usage rules.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"The quality of the samples were satisfactory.","option_b":"The quality of the samples was satisfactory.","option_c":"Despite of the delay, the quality of the samples was satisfactory.","option_d":"The team discussed about why the quality of the samples was satisfactory."},{"id":"ev-grammar-24","category":"english_verbal","subcategory":"Grammar","question_text":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","options":["She explained the procedure clear and concise.","Despite of the delay, she explained the procedure clearly and concisely.","She explained the procedure clearly and concisely.","The team discussed about why she explained the procedure clearly and concisely."],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The first sentence follows standard agreement, tense and usage rules.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"She explained the procedure clear and concise.","option_b":"Despite of the delay, she explained the procedure clearly and concisely.","option_c":"She explained the procedure clearly and concisely.","option_d":"The team discussed about why she explained the procedure clearly and concisely."},{"id":"ev-grammar-25","category":"english_verbal","subcategory":"Grammar","question_text":"Choose the grammatically correct sentence.","options":["By next June, they will completed the project.","Despite of the delay, by next june, they will have completed the project.","The team discussed about why by next june, they will have completed the project.","By next June, they will have completed the project."],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"The first sentence follows standard agreement, tense and usage rules.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"By next June, they will completed the project.","option_b":"Despite of the delay, by next june, they will have completed the project.","option_c":"The team discussed about why by next june, they will have completed the project.","option_d":"By next June, they will have completed the project."},{"id":"db-1-1","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"SQL Filtering","question_text":"Which clause filters rows before grouping?","options":["WHERE","HAVING","ORDER BY","DISTINCT"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"WHERE filters input rows; HAVING filters groups.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":1,"option_a":"WHERE","option_b":"HAVING","option_c":"ORDER BY","option_d":"DISTINCT"},{"id":"db-1-2","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Aggregation","question_text":"Which clause filters aggregate groups?","options":["WHERE","HAVING","SELECT DISTINCT","LIMIT"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"HAVING is evaluated on groups after GROUP BY.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":1,"option_a":"WHERE","option_b":"HAVING","option_c":"SELECT DISTINCT","option_d":"LIMIT"},{"id":"db-1-3","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Keys","question_text":"What property must every primary-key value have?","options":["It may repeat","It must be text","It is unique and non-null","It must reference another table"],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A primary key uniquely identifies a row and cannot be null.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"It may repeat","option_b":"It must be text","option_c":"It is unique and non-null","option_d":"It must reference another table"},{"id":"db-1-4","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Foreign Keys","question_text":"What does a foreign key primarily enforce?","options":["Sort order","Column encryption","Query caching","Referential integrity"],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"It constrains values to referenced candidate-key values.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Sort order","option_b":"Column encryption","option_c":"Query caching","option_d":"Referential integrity"},{"id":"db-1-5","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Normalization","question_text":"Which normal form removes partial dependency on a composite key?","options":["Second normal form","First normal form","Third normal form","BCNF only"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"2NF removes partial dependencies on a candidate key.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Second normal form","option_b":"First normal form","option_c":"Third normal form","option_d":"BCNF only"},{"id":"db-1-6","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Transactions","question_text":"Which ACID property ensures committed data survives failure?","options":["Atomicity","Durability","Isolation","Consistency"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Durability preserves committed changes.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Atomicity","option_b":"Durability","option_c":"Isolation","option_d":"Consistency"},{"id":"db-1-7","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Transactions","question_text":"Which ACID property makes a transaction all-or-nothing?","options":["Isolation","Durability","Atomicity","Availability"],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Atomicity prevents partial transactions.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Isolation","option_b":"Durability","option_c":"Atomicity","option_d":"Availability"},{"id":"db-1-8","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Indexes","question_text":"What is the usual trade-off of adding an index?","options":["Faster writes with no cost","Automatic normalization","Guaranteed uniqueness for every index","Faster reads but extra storage and write cost"],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Indexes accelerate matching reads but must be maintained on writes.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"Faster writes with no cost","option_b":"Automatic normalization","option_c":"Guaranteed uniqueness for every index","option_d":"Faster reads but extra storage and write cost"},{"id":"db-1-9","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"JOIN","question_text":"Which join retains every row from the left table?","options":["LEFT JOIN","INNER JOIN","CROSS JOIN","SELF JOIN"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"LEFT JOIN preserves all left rows.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"LEFT JOIN","option_b":"INNER JOIN","option_c":"CROSS JOIN","option_d":"SELF JOIN"},{"id":"db-1-10","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"NULL","question_text":"Which predicate correctly tests missing SQL values?","options":["= NULL","IS NULL","== NULL","NULL()"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"NULL uses IS NULL because equality with NULL is unknown.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"= NULL","option_b":"IS NULL","option_c":"== NULL","option_d":"NULL()"},{"id":"db-1-11","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Grouping","question_text":"A query selects department and COUNT(*). What else is normally required?","options":["ORDER BY department","HAVING department","GROUP BY department","DISTINCT COUNT"],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Non-aggregated selected columns normally belong in GROUP BY.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":1,"option_a":"ORDER BY department","option_b":"HAVING department","option_c":"GROUP BY department","option_d":"DISTINCT COUNT"},{"id":"db-1-12","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Isolation","question_text":"What anomaly is prevented when a transaction cannot read uncommitted changes?","options":["Lost index","Dead column","Schema drift","Dirty read"],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Dirty reads observe another transaction's uncommitted work.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":1,"option_a":"Lost index","option_b":"Dead column","option_c":"Schema drift","option_d":"Dirty read"},{"id":"db-1-13","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Query Logic","question_text":"COUNT(column_name) differs from COUNT(*) because it does what?","options":["Ignores NULL values in that column","Counts distinct values only","Counts tables","Returns text"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"COUNT(column) ignores NULL; COUNT(*) counts rows.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Ignores NULL values in that column","option_b":"Counts distinct values only","option_c":"Counts tables","option_d":"Returns text"},{"id":"db-1-14","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Set Operations","question_text":"What does UNION do by default?","options":["Keeps all duplicates","Removes duplicate rows","Joins columns by keys","Updates both queries"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"UNION applies duplicate elimination; UNION ALL does not.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Keeps all duplicates","option_b":"Removes duplicate rows","option_c":"Joins columns by keys","option_d":"Updates both queries"},{"id":"db-1-15","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Subqueries","question_text":"A correlated subquery is evaluated with reference to what?","options":["Only system tables","A stored procedure","A row from the outer query","An index page"],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"It references values supplied by the outer query.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Only system tables","option_b":"A stored procedure","option_c":"A row from the outer query","option_d":"An index page"},{"id":"db-1-16","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Constraints","question_text":"Which constraint limits values using a Boolean condition?","options":["DEFAULT","INDEX","VIEW","CHECK"],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"CHECK validates a predicate for inserted or updated rows.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"DEFAULT","option_b":"INDEX","option_c":"VIEW","option_d":"CHECK"},{"id":"db-1-17","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Views","question_text":"A standard database view primarily stores what?","options":["A query definition","A copy of every result row","An index only","Transaction logs"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"A normal view stores its defining query, unlike a materialized view.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"A query definition","option_b":"A copy of every result row","option_c":"An index only","option_d":"Transaction logs"},{"id":"db-1-18","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Normalization","question_text":"A transitive dependency of a non-key attribute violates which normal form?","options":["First normal form","Third normal form","Second normal form only","Domain-key normal form only"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"3NF removes transitive dependencies on candidate keys.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"First normal form","option_b":"Third normal form","option_c":"Second normal form only","option_d":"Domain-key normal form only"},{"id":"db-1-19","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Joins","question_text":"What does an INNER JOIN return?","options":["Every left row","Every possible pair only","Rows satisfying the join condition in both inputs","Only unmatched rows"],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"INNER JOIN retains matching combinations.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"Every left row","option_b":"Every possible pair only","option_c":"Rows satisfying the join condition in both inputs","option_d":"Only unmatched rows"},{"id":"db-1-20","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Ordering","question_text":"Without ORDER BY, which claim about result order is correct?","options":["Primary-key order is guaranteed","Insertion order is guaranteed","Alphabetic order is guaranteed","No order is guaranteed"],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Relational results are unordered unless ORDER BY is specified.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"Primary-key order is guaranteed","option_b":"Insertion order is guaranteed","option_c":"Alphabetic order is guaranteed","option_d":"No order is guaranteed"},{"id":"db-2-1","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Cardinality","question_text":"Which relationship lets one department contain many employees while each employee belongs to one department?","options":["One-to-many","One-to-one","Many-to-many","Self-reference"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"One-to-many models one parent row with multiple child rows.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":1,"option_a":"One-to-many","option_b":"One-to-one","option_c":"Many-to-many","option_d":"Self-reference"},{"id":"db-2-2","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Composite Keys","question_text":"What is a composite primary key?","options":["A key copied into two tables","A key formed from more than one column","An encrypted key","A nullable alternate key"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"A composite key uses multiple columns together to identify a row.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":1,"option_a":"A key copied into two tables","option_b":"A key formed from more than one column","option_c":"An encrypted key","option_d":"A nullable alternate key"},{"id":"db-2-3","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Candidate Keys","question_text":"What is a candidate key?","options":["Any indexed column","A foreign key awaiting approval","A minimal set of columns that uniquely identifies a row","A generated display label"],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"A candidate key is both unique and minimal.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Any indexed column","option_b":"A foreign key awaiting approval","option_c":"A minimal set of columns that uniquely identifies a row","option_d":"A generated display label"},{"id":"db-2-4","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Referential Actions","question_text":"What does ON DELETE CASCADE do?","options":["Prevents every parent deletion","Sets every child value to zero","Deletes the entire schema","Deletes dependent child rows when the referenced parent is deleted"],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"CASCADE propagates the parent deletion to referencing rows.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"Prevents every parent deletion","option_b":"Sets every child value to zero","option_c":"Deletes the entire schema","option_d":"Deletes dependent child rows when the referenced parent is deleted"},{"id":"db-2-5","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Aggregation","question_text":"Which expression counts distinct department values?","options":["COUNT(DISTINCT department)","DISTINCT(COUNT department)","COUNT(UNIQUE department)","SUM(DISTINCT department)"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"COUNT with DISTINCT counts unique non-null values.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"COUNT(DISTINCT department)","option_b":"DISTINCT(COUNT department)","option_c":"COUNT(UNIQUE department)","option_d":"SUM(DISTINCT department)"},{"id":"db-2-6","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Grouping","question_text":"Which clause filters groups after aggregation?","options":["WHERE","HAVING","ORDER BY","LIMIT"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"HAVING evaluates conditions on grouped results.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"WHERE","option_b":"HAVING","option_c":"ORDER BY","option_d":"LIMIT"},{"id":"db-2-7","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Sorting","question_text":"How do you sort salary from highest to lowest?","options":["ORDER salary REVERSE","SORT salary DOWN","ORDER BY salary DESC","GROUP BY salary DESC"],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"DESC requests descending order in ORDER BY.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":2,"option_a":"ORDER salary REVERSE","option_b":"SORT salary DOWN","option_c":"ORDER BY salary DESC","option_d":"GROUP BY salary DESC"},{"id":"db-2-8","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Transactions","question_text":"What does ROLLBACK do in an open transaction?","options":["Makes changes permanent","Creates an index","Locks the database forever","Discards uncommitted changes"],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"ROLLBACK reverses the current transaction's uncommitted work.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"Makes changes permanent","option_b":"Creates an index","option_c":"Locks the database forever","option_d":"Discards uncommitted changes"},{"id":"db-2-9","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Transactions","question_text":"What does COMMIT do?","options":["Makes the current transaction's changes permanent","Removes the current table","Cancels the current query plan","Restores a backup"],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"COMMIT completes a transaction and persists its changes.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"Makes the current transaction's changes permanent","option_b":"Removes the current table","option_c":"Cancels the current query plan","option_d":"Restores a backup"},{"id":"db-2-10","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Isolation","question_text":"What is a non-repeatable read?","options":["Reading a row with duplicate columns","Reading the same row twice and seeing different committed values","Running the same query on two databases","Reading without an index"],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Another committed transaction can change a row between two reads.","estimated_time_seconds":60,"difficulty":3,"option_a":"Reading a row with duplicate columns","option_b":"Reading the same row twice and seeing different committed values","option_c":"Running the same query on two databases","option_d":"Reading without an index"},{"id":"db-2-11","category":"database_technical","subcategory":"Indexes","question_text":"Why can too many indexes slow INSERT operations?","options":["Indexes disable transactions","Every index duplicates the database",